  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe 00z sounding Saturday afternoon shows a well mixed marine \nboundary layer, around 2500 feet deep with an inversion up to 5,000 \nfeet and very dry air within and above it. Patchy low clouds are \nexpected to fill over the lowlands of San Diego and Orange Counties \nas well as parts of the Inland Empire through this morning. The \nclosed upper low continues to approach our region through Monday,\ndeepening the marine layer to about 4000-5000 feet tonight into \nearly Monday and significantly improving minimum humidities Monday\nafternoon across mountains (12-25% this afternoon to 25-50% \nMonday) and deserts (10-15% this afternoon to 18-30% Monday). With\nthe deepening marine layer, there is a moderate chance for \ndrizzle in the coastal basin early Monday and early Tuesday.\n\nMonday will also likely be the coolest and breeziest day of the\nweek as the main trough axis passes through the region. W/SW \nwinds will be slightly elevated Monday afternoon and evening over \ndesert slopes, gusting 20-35 mph and higher through the Banning \nPass. Temperatures will likely struggle to reach the 80s across \nthe Coastal Basin with perhaps a few low-80s in the IE while low \ndesert temperatures are likely (80% chance) to remain below 100F\nboth Monday AND Tuesday. \n\nAs the closed low departs the region, the longwave background ridge \nacross the Southern US replaces the low and fills in over SoCal, \nimmediately spiking the temperatures back to near average for \ninland areas for Wednesday, a jump of nearly 8-12F. That \ncorresponds to highs within a few degrees of 80 at the coast, \nupper 80s inland, mid-90s in the IE and low-100s in the lower \ndeserts Wednesday through Friday.\n\nAt this time, both the ECMWF and GFS are in agreement that a trough \nwill move through NorCal and the PNW next weekend, though it \nremains uncertain how much it will have an influence on \ntemperatures in SoCal.",
